# NorthNarrow — process comm allowlist OPERATOR OVERLAY (example)
#
# Beta Step 3. This is a TEMPLATE. To activate it, copy it to the live
# overlay path and edit:
#
#     cp /etc/northnarrow/process-comm-allowlist.local.example \
#        /etc/northnarrow/process-comm-allowlist.local
#
# The agent merges this overlay on top of the shipped
# `process-comm-allowlist.v1` default at boot. Unlike the `.v1` file,
# entries here carry a prefix:
#
#     +name   ADD a program to the allowlist
#     -name   RE-ENABLE detection on a program that `.v1` allowlists
#
# What a match does (external audit PROC-008; see the `.v1` header):
#   - as an ACTOR, R011/R012/R013/R017 record (Log) instead of killing a
#     root exec of a system-tree program that runs under it — add the
#     automation that runs the flagged tools (`+my-deploy-tool`);
#   - as a TARGET, the program itself draws no verdict from R006, R007,
#     R011..R017 or the io_uring and BPF rules, anywhere on the host.
#
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored. A name is matched on what
# the kernel executed: the executable's basename, or the script's an
# interpreter runs, inside a root-owned system tree (/usr, /opt, /bin,
# /sbin, /lib, /snap, /nix/store) — never on the name a process gives
# itself. The kernel's 15-character form of a longer name still works
# (`containerd-shim` covers `containerd-shim-runc-v2`). Every change
# here is logged at boot.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONTAINER RUNTIMES — uncomment ONLY on a host that runs containers
# (Docker, Kubernetes, podman, containerd, CRI-O).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SECURITY IMPLICATION — READ BEFORE ENABLING:
#
#   R013 (Namespace/escape tooling, MITRE T1611 Escape-to-Host) fires
#   when `runc` execs from a NON-standard path (a dropped/modified runc
#   in /tmp, /dev/shm, a writable bundle dir, …) — the exact shape of
#   the CVE-2019-5736 runc-overwrite breakout. Adding `+runc` below is a
#   TARGET entry: it exempts every `runc` in a root-owned system tree
#   (/usr/local, /opt, …) from R013 on this host; a copy in a writable
#   path is still judged. Only enable it where legitimate container
#   activity is producing R013 false positives.
#
#   Note: of the comms below, only `runc` is currently an R013 target;
#   the others never trip R013 and are listed for parity / forward
#   compatibility with future container-aware rules. They are harmless
#   to enable but also unnecessary for silencing R013 today.
#
# Uncomment the lines that match your runtime:
#
#+runc
#+crun
#+containerd-shim
#+conmon
#+docker-init
#+dumb-init

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SITE-SPECIFIC EXEMPTIONS — add trusted ACTORS here (the automation, not
# the tool it runs), each with a comment explaining WHY it is trusted (so
# the next operator/auditor understands the exemption).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Example:
#   +my-deploy-tool   # in-house provisioning agent, sets caps on deploy
